theory.intro:
You can estimate an angle's size by comparing it with familiar **benchmark** angles before measuring.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles,quotes,calc}
\coordinate (V1) at (0,0);\coordinate (A1) at (1.6,0);\coordinate (B1) at (45:1.6);
\draw (A1)--(V1)--(B1);
\pic[draw,"$45^{\circ}$",angle radius=0.45cm,angle eccentricity=2.4]{angle=A1--V1--B1};
\node[font=\small] at (0.8,-0.8) {half a right angle};
\coordinate (V2) at (4.4,0);\coordinate (A2) at (6,0);\coordinate (B2) at (4.4,1.6);
\draw (A2)--(V2)--(B2);
\pic[draw,"$90^{\circ}$",angle radius=0.45cm,angle eccentricity=2.4]{angle=A2--V2--B2};
\node[font=\small] at (5.2,-0.8) {right angle};
\coordinate (V3) at (1.6,-3.2);\coordinate (A3) at (3.2,-3.2);\coordinate (B3) at (0,-3.2);
\draw (A3)--(V3)--(B3);\fill (V3) circle (1.5pt);
\pic[draw,"$180^{\circ}$",angle radius=0.45cm,angle eccentricity=2.2]{angle=A3--V3--B3};
\node[font=\small] at (1.6,-4.0) {straight angle};
\coordinate (V4) at (5.2,-3.2);
\draw (V4)--($(V4)+(1.4,0)$);\fill (V4) circle (1.5pt);
\draw[->] ($(V4)+(12:0.7)$) arc (12:348:0.7);
\node[font=\small] at (5.2,-4.0) {full turn $360^{\circ}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
